QB.add(47,[{"n":1,"q":"A 45-year-old woman presents with fatigue, pallor, and mild jaundice. Blood tests reveal a macrocytic anaemia with a mean cell volume (MCV) of 110 fL. Further testing showed elevated bilirubin and high lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), and peripheral blood smear showed the following: What is the most likely cause of her anaemia?","o":[{"t":"Myelodysplasia"},{"t":"Vitamin B12 deficiency"},{"t":"Alcohol-induced liver disease"},{"t":"Reticulocytosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The most likely cause of her anaemia is megaloblastic anaemia due to vitamin B12 or folate deficiency . This is suggested by macrocytic anaemia, hypersegmented neutrophils, and elevated bilirubin and LDH."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Macrocytic Anaemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Characterised by larger-than-normal red blood cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mean cell volume (MCV) > 100 fL."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology: Defective DNA Synthesis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Defective DNA synthesis in vitamin B12 or folate deficiency leads to: Nucleocytoplasmic asynchrony: Cells show arrested nuclear maturation but normal cytoplasmic development."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nucleocytoplasmic asynchrony: Cells show arrested nuclear maturation but normal cytoplasmic development."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nucleocytoplasmic asynchrony: Cells show arrested nuclear maturation but normal cytoplasmic development."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Megaloblastic Anaemia (Subtype of Macrocytic Anaemia):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Caused by vitamin B12 or folate deficiency ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Key features include: Ineffective erythropoiesis : Arrested cell development and intramedullary haemolysis. High bilirubin and LDH : Due to haemolysis in the bone marrow. Elevated iron stores : Iron not used effectively for haemoglobin production. Large, oval red cells : Sometimes with nuclear remnants. Giant metamyelocytes : Immature granulocyte precursors with large, sausage-shaped nuclei. Hypersegmented neutrophils : Mature neutrophils with 6+ nuclear lobes. Pancytopenia : Deficiency of all blood cell types (red cells, white cells, platelets) in severe cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ineffective erythropoiesis : Arrested cell development and intramedullary haemolysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High bilirubin and LDH : Due to haemolysis in the bone marrow."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated iron stores : Iron not used effectively for haemoglobin production."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Large, oval red cells : Sometimes with nuclear remnants."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Giant metamyelocytes : Immature granulocyte precursors with large, sausage-shaped nuclei."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypersegmented neutrophils : Mature neutrophils with 6+ nuclear lobes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pancytopenia : Deficiency of all blood cell types (red cells, white cells, platelets) in severe cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ineffective erythropoiesis : Arrested cell development and intramedullary haemolysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High bilirubin and LDH : Due to haemolysis in the bone marrow."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated iron stores : Iron not used effectively for haemoglobin production."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Large, oval red cells : Sometimes with nuclear remnants."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Giant metamyelocytes : Immature granulocyte precursors with large, sausage-shaped nuclei."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypersegmented neutrophils : Mature neutrophils with 6+ nuclear lobes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pancytopenia : Deficiency of all blood cell types (red cells, white cells, platelets) in severe cases."},{"k":"img","v":7204},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of Macrocytic Anaemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vitamin B12 or folate deficiency"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myelodysplasia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Liver disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alcohol consumption"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypothyroidism"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Drugs (e.g. zidovudine, trimethoprim, phenytoin, methotrexate, hydroxycarbamide)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reticulocytosis : Increased reticulocytes (larger, young red cells) due to: Haemolysis Increased red cell production in response to anaemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Haemolysis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased red cell production in response to anaemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Haemolysis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased red cell production in response to anaemia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Myelodysplasia (Option A) : While it can cause macrocytosis, the typical blood smear findings and vitamin B12 deficiency favour a diagnosis of megaloblastic anaemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Alcohol-induced liver disease (Option C) : Can cause macrocytosis but does not explain the hypersegmented neutrophils and elevated LDH."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Reticulocytosis (Option D) : Would cause macrocytosis, but there is no evidence of increased reticulocytes or haemolysis."}],"qi":[7204]},{"n":2,"q":"Match the following: a. Bernard soulier syndrome 1. antibodies to, the metalloprotease ADAMTS13 b. Glanzmann’s thrombasthenia 2. absence of the platelet Gp Ib-IX-V receptor c. Hemolytic uremic syndrome 3. absence of the platelet Gp IIb/IIIa receptor d. Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura 4. Escherichia coli O157:H7\na. Bernard soulier syndrome 1. antibodies to, the metalloprotease ADAMTS13\nb. Glanzmann’s thrombasthenia 2. absence of the platelet Gp Ib-IX-V receptor\nc. Hemolytic uremic syndrome 3. absence of the platelet Gp IIb/IIIa receptor d. Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura 4. Escherichia coli O157:H7","o":[{"t":"a-1, b-2, c-3, d-4"},{"t":"a-2, b-3, c-4, d-1"},{"t":"a-3, b-4, c-1, d-2"},{"t":"a-4, b-1, c-2, d-3"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"a. Bernard soulier syndrome 2. absence of the platelet Gp Ib-IX-V receptor"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"b. Glanzmann’s thrombasthenia 3. absence of the platelet Gp IIb/IIIa receptor"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"c. Hemolytic uremic syndrome 4. Escherichia coli O157:H7"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"d. Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura 1. antibodies to, the metalloprotease ADAMTS13"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Glanzmann’s thrombasthenia (absence of the platelet Gp IIb/IIIa receptor) and Bernard-Soulier syndrome (absence of the platelet Gp Ib-IX-V receptor). Both are inherited in an autosomal recessive fashion and present with bleeding symptoms in childhood."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura : The pathogenesis of inherited (Upshaw-Schulman syndrome) and idiopathic TTP (ITTP) is related to a deficiency of, or antibodies to, the metalloprotease ADAMTS13, which cleaves VWF."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"VWF is normally secreted as ultra-large multimers, which are then cleaved by ADAMTS13."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The persistence of ultra-large VWF molecules is thought to contribute to pathogenic platelet adhesion and aggregation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Therapeutic plasma exchange (TPE) remains the mainstay of treatment of TTP. TPE is continued until the platelet count is normal and signs of hemolysis are resolved for at least 2 days."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) is a syndrome characterized by acute renal failure, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, and thrombocytopenia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is seen preceded by an episode of diarrhea, often hemorrhagic in nature, predominantly in children."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Escherichia coli O157:H7 is the most frequent etiologic serotype."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atypical HUS (aHUS) is usually due to genetic defects in complement genes or antibodies directed against complementary regulatory proteins that result in chronic complement activation."}]},{"n":3,"q":"Match the following: a) Hemophilia A 1. Factor IX deficiency b) Von-Willebrand disease 2. Factor XIII deficiency c) Osler weber rendu disease 3. Factor VIII deficiency d) Laki lorand factor 4. Arteriovenous malformations e) Hemophilia B 5. Von willebrand factor deficiency\na) Hemophilia A 1. Factor IX deficiency\nb) Von-Willebrand disease 2. Factor XIII deficiency\nc) Osler weber rendu disease 3. Factor VIII deficiency\nd) Laki lorand factor 4. Arteriovenous malformations\ne) Hemophilia B 5. Von willebrand factor deficiency","o":[{"t":"a-2, b-5, c-1, d-3, e-4"},{"t":"a-1, b-5, c-2, d-4, e-3"},{"t":"a-4 , b-5, c-3, d-1, e-2"},{"t":"a-3, b-5, c-4, d-2, e-1"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hemophilia A 3. Factor VIII deficiency"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Von-Willebrand disease 5. Von willebrand factor deficiency"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Osler weber rendu disease 4. Arteriovenous malformations"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Laki lorand factor 2. Factor XIII deficiency"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hemophilia B 1. Factor IX deficiency"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1. Von Willebrand Disease : Autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in vWF, a large protein that promotes the adhesion of platelets to subendothelial collagen and stabilizes factor VIII"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Type 1 and type 3 von Willebrand disease are associated with quantitative defects in vWF"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type 1, an autosomal dominant disorder characterized by a mild to moderate vWF deficiency. It is associated with point substitutions that interfere with maturation of the vWF protein or that result in rapid clearance from the plasma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Type 3 disease is an autosomal disorder usually caused by deletions or frameshift mutations resulting in little to no vWF synthesis. Because vWF stabilizes factor VIII in the circulation, factor VIII levels are also reduced in type 3 disease and the associated bleeding disorder is often severe."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Type 2 von Willebrand disease is characterized by qualitative defects in vWF ; there are several subtypes, of which type 2A is the most common . It is inherited as an autosomal dominant disorder."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2. Hemophilia A , the most common hereditary disease associated with life-threatening bleeding, is caused by mutations in factor VIII , an essential cofactor for factor IX in the coagulation cascade."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemophilia A is inherited as an X-linked recessive trait and thus affects mainly males and homozygous females."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients with hemophilia A have a prolonged PTT and a normal PT, results that point to an abnormality of the intrinsic coagulation pathway."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Factor VIII–specific assays are required for diagnosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemophilia A is treated with infusions of recombinant factor VIII."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"3. Hemophilia B (Christmas Disease, Factor IX Deficiency) Severe factor IX deficiency produces a disorder clinically indistinguishable from factor VIII deficiency"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"it is inherited as an X-linked recessive trait"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The PTT is prolonged and the PT is normal."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis of Christmas disease is possible only by assay of the factor levels. The disease is treated with infusions of recombinant factor IX."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"4. Osler weber rendu disease / Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT) is characterized by the presence of multiple arteriovenous malformations (AVMs) that lack intervening capillaries and result in direct connections between arteries and veins."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most common clinical manifestation is spontaneous and recurrent nosebleeds (epistaxis) beginning on average at age 12 years."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Telangiectasia (small AVMs) are characteristically found on the lips, tongue, buccal and gastrointestinal (GI) mucosa, face, and fingers."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"5. Factor XIII (FXIII) deficiency / Laki lorand factor deficiency is a rare bleeding disorder that presents with symptoms spanning from delayed umbilical cord separation to intracranial hemorrhage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"FXIII plays a pivotal role in stabilizing clots and facilitating the cross-linking of the fibrin polymer, thereby ensuring effective hemostasis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The acquired FXIII deficiency typically arises from factors such as hyperconsumption, hemodilution, and decreased synthesis and is more prevalent than the congenital form."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In rare instances, individuals with acquired FXIII deficiency may generate autoantibodies targeting FXIII subunits."}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 34-year-old male is being treated for tuberculosis with rifampin, isoniazid, and pyrazinamide. He has been on this regimen for the past 4 weeks. Over the last few days, he has noticed easy bruising, petechiae on his legs, and gum bleeding. He denies any history of trauma and he does not have any signs of active bleeding. On examination, he has petechiae on his extremities and ecchymosis. Laboratory tests show: Platelet count: 45,000/µL (normal range: 150,000–450,000/µL) Hemoglobin: 13.5 g/dL (normal) White blood cell count: Normal Peripheral blood smear: No signs of platelet clumping, but reduced platelet count Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?","o":[{"t":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT)"},{"t":"Drug-induced thrombocytopenia due to rifampin"},{"t":"Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)"},{"t":"Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient is presenting with thrombocytopenia (low platelet count) and a history of rifampin usage for tuberculosis treatment, which strongly suggests drug-induced thrombocytopenia ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Drugs can induce thrombocytopenia through direct effects on platelets and secondary to immunologically mediated platelet destruction. The drugs most commonly implicated are quinine, quinidine, and vancomycin, all of which induce drug-dependent antibody binding to platelet glycoproteins."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinically, these patients present with moderate to severe thrombocytopenia, and spontaneous bleeding varying from simple ecchymoses, petechiae and mucosal bleeding to life-threatening spontaneous intracranial hemorrhage."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"There are two types of drug-induced thrombocytopenia: immune and nonimmune."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If a medicine causes your immune system to produce antibodies which seek and destroy your platelets, the condition is called drug-induced immune thrombocytopenia . Heparin , a blood thinner, is the most common cause of drug-induced immune thrombocytopenia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If a medicine prevents your bone marrow from making enough platelets, the condition is called drug-induced non immune thrombocytopenia . Chemotherapy drugs and a seizure medicine called valproic acid may lead to this problem."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Other medicines that cause drug-induced thrombocytopenia include:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Furosemide"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gold, used to treat arthritis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Penicillin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Quinidine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Quinine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ranitidine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sulfonamides"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Linezolid and other antibiotics"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Statins"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rifampin"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The first step is to stop using the medicine that is causing the problem."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"For people who have life-threatening bleeding, treatments may include:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immunoglobulin therapy (IVIG)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Plasma exchange (plasmapheresis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Platelet transfusions"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Corticosteroid medicine"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) (Option A) :HIT is a well-known cause of thrombocytopenia that occurs after exposure to heparin . It is caused by the formation of antibodies against the heparin-platelet factor 4 complex , which activates platelets and leads tothrombosis and thrombocytopenia ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) (Option C) :ITP is an autoimmune disorder in which the body's immune system produces antibodies against platelets, leading to destruction of platelets in the spleen. The most common presenting symptoms are petechiae , easy bruising , and gum bleeding ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) (Option D) : The classic findings of DIC include low platelet count , prolonged PT and aPTT , increased D-dimer , and organ dysfunction (e.g., renal or liver failure). This patient does not have any clinical signs of sepsis , trauma , or other common triggers of DIC"}]},{"n":5,"q":"Which of the following is a correct combination of PBF cells?","o":[{"t":"1) Monocyte, 2) Basophil, 3) Lymphocyte, 4) Eosinophil, 5) Neutrophil"},{"t":"1) Lymphocyte, 2) Eosinophil, 3) Basophil, 4) Neutrophil, 5) Monocyte"},{"t":"1) Eosinophil, 2) Basophil, 3) Monocyte, 4) Lymphocyte, 5) Neutrophil"},{"t":"1) Neutrophil, 2) Monocyte, 3) Lymphocyte, 4) Basophil, 5) Eosinophil"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This option accurately includes a normal representation of various white blood cells."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Normal Peripheral blood smear:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cell Type Description Slide"},{"k":"img","v":7288},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Red Blood Cells Round, biconcave shape"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Stain deeply with eosin Diameter: 6.0–8.5 mm Mostly uniform; small percentage may appear oval or irregular (schistocytes <0.1%) Size: 6.0–8.5 mm"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"WBC’s"},{"k":"img","v":7295},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Neutrophils Segmented nucleus (3–5 lobes) (Image 5) Pink/orange cytoplasm due to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"granules Diameter: ~13 mm Size: ~13 mm"},{"k":"img","v":7297},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Eosinophils Two lobed nucleus (Image 4) Packed with gold/orange granules"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Diameter: 12–17 mm Size: 12–17 mm"},{"k":"img","v":7302},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Basophils Compact, irregular nucleus (Image 2) Large purple granules"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Rare (<1% of leukocytes) Size: Variable (typically 12–15 mm)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":7308},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Monocytes Largest leukocytes (Image 1) Horseshoe-shaped nucleus"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Bluish-grey cytoplasm with fine granules Diameter: 15–18 mm Size:15–18 mm"},{"k":"img","v":7313},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lymphocytes Small cells with a thin rim of (Image 3) cytoplasm"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Uniform nuclei (~9 mm in diameter) About 85% are T cells or NK cells Size: ~9 mm"},{"k":"img","v":7318},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Platelets Irregular outline with fine red"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"granules Diameter: 1–3 mm (some larger platelets up to 5 mm may be present) Size: 1–3 mm (up to 5 mm)"}],"qi":[7212]},{"n":6,"q":"A 7-year-old boy presents with severe pain in his right elbow, which has persisted for the past two days. His mother reports that the pain is sharp and debilitating, causing him to wake up at night. On examination, he is pale and tachycardic, with a heart rate of 120 bpm. The right elbow shows swelling, tenderness, and warmth. A peripheral blood smear is performed as shown. Which of the following statements is incorrect for this condition?","o":[{"t":"Decrease reticulocytes in aplastic crisis"},{"t":"Howell-Jolly bodies may be present"},{"t":"Newborns are symptomatic"},{"t":"Hydration may relieve symptoms"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis is Sickle cell disease in painful vaso-occlusive crisis of the right elbow, as indicated by the peripheral blood film showing sickle cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Newborns are usually asymptomatic due to the presence of fetal hemoglobin (HbF) that is resistant to sickling and manifests after 6 months."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sickle-cell anaemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Genetics Autosomal recessive disorder; mutation in b-globin"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"chain (Glu fi Val); results in HbS instead of HbA."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Types Homozygote (HbSS): Sickle cell anemia"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Heterozygote (HbAS): Sickle cell trait (asymptomatic, protects against malaria)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiology Decreased RBC survival;HbS polymerises to form sticky"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"stiff Hb precipitates- causes sickling ; sickled cells cause vaso-occlusion, especially during dehydration or deoxygenation. Factors increasing sickling- Acidosis, hypoxia, dehydration"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Classic Presentation Sudden severe pain (chest, back, thigh); may include"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"fever. Acute chest syndrome: Chest pain, fever, wheezing, cough, tachypnea Common infections (S. pneumoniae, Mycoplasma, etc.) Other Vaso-occlusive crises: (triggered by infection, hypoxia, dehydration, cold) Dactylitis Mesenteric ischemia CNS infarction (stroke) Priapism Avascular necrosis (AVN) Renal papillary necrosis: decreased ability to concentrate urine; may present as polyuria/nocturia Anemia, jaundice, pigmented cholelithiasis Osteomyelitis (S. aureus, Salmonella). ... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":7324},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diagnosis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management of Hydroxyurea Chronic Disease Increases fetal hemoglobin"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Monitor WBCs. Chronic transfusion therapy Risk of iron overload. Folic acid supplementation Prevents macrocytic anemia. HSCT (TOC)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management of Crises Pain management"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"O2 therapy IV fluid rehydration (Option D) Antibiotics if infection suspected. For VOCs progressing to acute chest syndrome Aggressive hydration, antibiotics, incentive spirometry, transfusions if needed."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Prophylaxis Prophylactic pneumococcal vaccination and antibiotics"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"for patients with auto splenectomy Cholecystectomy for recurrent cholelithiasis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Sickle Cell Trait (HbAS) Generally benign"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Complications include hematuria, renal concentrating defect, and increased UTI risk."}],"qi":[7215]},{"n":7,"q":"A 68-year-old male presents with fatigue, weight loss, numbness in his fingers, and episodes of cold-induced color changes. A bone marrow biopsy shows greater than 10% small lymphocytes and a finding as shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Multiple myeloma"},{"t":"Waldenström macroglobulinemia"},{"t":"Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance"},{"t":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most likely diagnosis is Waldenström Macroglobulinemia. Presents with fatigue, weight loss, numbness in his fingers, and episodes of cold-induced color changes. A bone marrow biopsy shows greater than 10% small lymphocytes and intranuclear pseudo-inclusions known as Dutcher bodies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presents with fatigue, weight loss, numbness in his fingers, and episodes of cold-induced color changes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A bone marrow biopsy shows greater than 10% small lymphocytes and intranuclear pseudo-inclusions known as Dutcher bodies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presents with fatigue, weight loss, numbness in his fingers, and episodes of cold-induced color changes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A bone marrow biopsy shows greater than 10% small lymphocytes and intranuclear pseudo-inclusions known as Dutcher bodies."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Key feature B cell malignancy s MYD88 mutation"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Increased levels of IgM (Vs increase igA & igG in MM) MGUS is the precursor just as in multiple myeloma"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Symptoms Lethargy, weight loss, raynaud phenomenon, peripheral"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"neuropathy Hyperviscosity syndrome: Headache, blurry vision, retinal hemorrhages No CRAB symptoms (hyper C alcemia, r enal involvement, a nemia, b one lytic lesions) Vs MM has CRAB symptoms (Option A)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Complicatio Thrombosis ns Organomegaly and dysfunction"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Cryoglobulinemia Cold agglutinin disease (AIHA) Amyloidosis"},{"k":"img","v":7331},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Diagnosis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Bone marrow biopsy and aspirate (most accurate) > 10% small lymphocytes Intranuclear cytoplasmic pseudo-inclusions (Dutcher bodies) Dutcher bodies (PAS-positive IgM deposits)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Treatment Rituximab"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Chemotherapy Plasmapheresis (for hyperviscosity symptoms)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Note:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Plasma cell inclusion body"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dutcher bodies: intranuclear"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Russel bodies: Intracytoplasmic"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mott cell: Multiple russel bodies"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (Option C): MGUS is usually asymptomatic and characterized by less than 10% plasma cells in the bone marrow."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (Option D): is characterized by the accumulation of small, mature lymphocytes and is often associated with smudge cells on peripheral blood smear. The presence of Dutcher bodies and the patient's peripheral neuropathy, along with cold-induced color changes, are not typical for CLL"}],"qi":[7217]},{"n":8,"q":"Which of the following statements regarding the PBF shown is incorrect?","o":[{"t":"It is associated with an autoimmune condition"},{"t":"It is associated with a hereditary condition"},{"t":"Cells show an increase in surface area-to-volume ratio"},{"t":"Mycoplasma pneumonia may be a cause"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Spherocytes are seen in the peripheral blood smear. These are small, spherical cells without central pallor. Surface area-to-volume ratio is decreased due to the loss of membrane surface area."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Spherocytes:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Definition Small, spherical cells without central pallor."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Surface area-to-volume ratio: decreased due to loss of membrane surface area. (Option C) Peripheral smear\" data-author=\"Paulo Henrique Orlandi Mourao\" data-hash=\"9767\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 3.0 \" data-source=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spherocytosis #/media/File:Hereditary_Spherocytosis_smear_2010-03- 17.JPG\" data-tags=\"April2025\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"HcDwlQcR1744420900.png\" />"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Associated Hereditary Spherocytosis (Option A) Conditions Autoimmune Hemolytic Anemia (AIHA) (Option B) ,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"G6PD deficiency , burns , BT reactions"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Key tests Both conditions can present with spherocytes and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"positive osmotic fragility tests. Only AIHA will have a positive direct Coombs’s test."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Feature Hereditary Spherocytosis Autoimmune Hemolytic Anemia"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(AIHA)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Definitio Autosomal dominant defect in RBC Autoantibodies against RBC n membrane proteins (ankyrin>>spectr membrane causing extravascular"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"in) leading to spherical RBCs. hemolysis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Extravascular hemolytic anemia, Hemolytic anemia symptoms. Present splenomegaly, jaundice; risk of ation acute cholecystitis from pigmented"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"gallstones. Aplastic crisis: Parvovirus B19 Hemolytic crisis: EBV"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diagnos Best Initial Test: CBC (normal to low Direct Coombs Test: Positive in is MCV, › MCHC, n Coombs test) warm AIHA"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Blood Smear: Shows spherocytes Cold Agglutinin Test: Effective in Most Accurate Test: Eosin-5 cold AIHA. maleimide flow cytometry, acidified glycerol lysis test."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatme Splenectomy (stops hemolysis but Mild AIHA: No treatment necessary nt spherocytes remain) Warm AIHA: Steroids; splenectomy"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Chronic folic acid replacement for recurrent episodes (assists RBC production). Severe, non-responsive hemolysis:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"IVIG Severe cold AIHA: Keep warm, ± rituximab."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Types N/A Warm AIHA: IgG, associated with"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"SLE, CLL, lymphoma, some drugs. Cold AIHA: IgM, associated with Mycoplasma pneumonia, EBV, Waldenström macroglobulinemia (Option D)"}],"qi":[7220]},{"n":9,"q":"Which condition is most likely associated with the cell marked with an arrow in the blood film?","o":[{"t":"A) Aortic Stenosis"},{"t":"HELLP Syndrome"},{"t":"C) DIC"},{"t":"D) All of the above"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The image reveals Schistocytes (Helmet cells) which are fragmented red blood cell (RBC), typically irregular in shape with two pointed ends, smaller than normal RBCs, and variable in appearance with sharp angles or spines."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Commonly Associated Conditions"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Condition Type Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Microangiopathic Red blood cells (RBCs) are damaged as they pass Hemolytic Anemias through obstructed or narrowed blood vessels."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Causes intravascular hemolysis due to shear stress and mechanical damage. Causes: Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura (TTP) / Hemolytic Uremic Syndrome (HUS) Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) HELLP Syndrome Hypertensive Emergency"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Macroangiopathic A type of hemolytic anemia caused by mechanical hemolytic anemia destruction of red blood cells (RBCs)."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Causes: Prosthetic Heart Valves : Leads to turbulent blood flow and shear stress on RBCs, resulting in their destruction. Aortic Stenosis : Narrowing of the aortic valve leads to mechanical destruction of RBCs as they pass through the constricted area."},{"k":"img","v":7226}],"qi":[7226]},{"n":10,"q":"A 32-year-old man presents to the emergency department with fatigue, jaundice, and dark urine. A blood film reveals: The patient has a history of dapsone use for the management of a skin condition.Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of the bite cell formation in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Sickle cell disease"},{"t":"Vitamin B12 deficiency"},{"t":"Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency"},{"t":"Iron deficiency anaemia"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient likely has oxidative haemolysis due to glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency , as indicated by the presence of bite cells and polychromasia on the blood film following exposure to dapsone. This condition leads to the premature destruction of red blood cells and resultant haemolytic anaemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bite Cells:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bite cells are normal-sized red blood cells with a characteristic appearance, resembling cells with a 'bite' taken out of their membrane."},{"k":"img","v":7231},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Oxidative Haemolysis and Bite Cell Formation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Causes : Defects in red blood cell enzymes, particularly glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency. Exposure to oxidative drugs such as dapsone and sulfasalazine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Defects in red blood cell enzymes, particularly glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exposure to oxidative drugs such as dapsone and sulfasalazine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Defects in red blood cell enzymes, particularly glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exposure to oxidative drugs such as dapsone and sulfasalazine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oxidative damage causes RBC destruction via: Intravascular hemolysis (IVH): RBCs lyse directly in circulation. Extravascular hemolysis (EVH): Damaged RBCs are recognized and destroyed by macrophages in the reticuloendothelial system (RES) (e.g., spleen)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intravascular hemolysis (IVH): RBCs lyse directly in circulation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extravascular hemolysis (EVH): Damaged RBCs are recognized and destroyed by macrophages in the reticuloendothelial system (RES) (e.g., spleen)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intravascular hemolysis (IVH): RBCs lyse directly in circulation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extravascular hemolysis (EVH): Damaged RBCs are recognized and destroyed by macrophages in the reticuloendothelial system (RES) (e.g., spleen)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mechanism : G6PD deficiency makes red blood cells susceptible to oxidative damage. Under oxidative stress, haemoglobin denatures, forming Heinz bodies that adhere to the membrane. Spleen macrophages attempt to remove Heinz bodies, resulting in the formation of bite cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"G6PD deficiency makes red blood cells susceptible to oxidative damage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Under oxidative stress, haemoglobin denatures, forming Heinz bodies that adhere to the membrane."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spleen macrophages attempt to remove Heinz bodies, resulting in the formation of bite cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"G6PD deficiency makes red blood cells susceptible to oxidative damage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Under oxidative stress, haemoglobin denatures, forming Heinz bodies that adhere to the membrane."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spleen macrophages attempt to remove Heinz bodies, resulting in the formation of bite cells."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Identifying Bite Cells and Diagnosing Oxidative Haemolysis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood Film Findings : Presence of bite cells. Blister cells: Red blood cells with blistering on the membrane. Irregularly shaped small cells. Polychromasia: Increased young red blood cells (reticulocytes) indicating heightened red cell production."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presence of bite cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blister cells: Red blood cells with blistering on the membrane."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Irregularly shaped small cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Polychromasia: Increased young red blood cells (reticulocytes) indicating heightened red cell production."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic Tools : Supravital stain (e.g., methyl violet) to visualize Heinz bodies ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Supravital stain (e.g., methyl violet) to visualize Heinz bodies ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presence of bite cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blister cells: Red blood cells with blistering on the membrane."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Irregularly shaped small cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Polychromasia: Increased young red blood cells (reticulocytes) indicating heightened red cell production."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Supravital stain (e.g., methyl violet) to visualize Heinz bodies ."},{"k":"img","v":7341},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Measuring G6PD levels to confirm deficiency : Meth Hb reduction test."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Measuring G6PD levels to confirm deficiency : Meth Hb reduction test."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Measuring G6PD levels to confirm deficiency : Meth Hb reduction test."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Significance of Bite Cells:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bite cells indicate oxidative damage and premature destruction of red blood cells.(usually affects older RBC’s)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This can lead to haemolytic anaemia, with severity ranging from mild to life-threatening."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sickle cell disease (Option A) : While it causes hemolysis, it is not typically associated with bite cells."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Vitamin B12 deficiency (Option B) : This causes macrocytic anaemia and does not lead to bite cell formation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Iron deficiency anaemia (Option D) : This condition typically presents with microcytic cells and does not produce bite cells."}],"qi":[7231]},{"n":11,"q":"A 60-year-old man with non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) is undergoing evaluation for his treatment response. His healthcare provider orders a scan. Which of the following statements best describes the role of the above scan in the management of NHL?","o":[{"t":"Bone scans are primarily used for initial diagnosis and do not provide information on treatment response."},{"t":"PET scans can assess metabolic activity to help stage the disease and monitor treatment effectiveness."},{"t":"Gallium-67 scans are useful for detecting recurrence of lymphoma."},{"t":"Tc-99m tilmanocept scans require preparation and cannot be performed immediately after a meal."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"PET scans are crucial in the management of non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) as they evaluate the metabolic activity of lymph nodes. This helps in accurately staging the disease by identifying areas of increased metabolic activity, indicating cancer involvement. Additionally, PET scans are used to monitor treatment response by assessing changes in metabolic activity post-therapy, differentiating between active disease and residual effects like scar tissue."},{"k":"img","v":7237},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Purpose of PET Scans in NHL:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aspect Details"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Staging Assess disease spread by identifying areas of increased"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"metabolic activity indicating cancer."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Evaluation Differentiate between active cancer cells and scar tissue"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"after treatment."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Detection of Monitor for potential lymphoma recurrence, guiding Recurrence further management."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mechanism: It utilizes 18F-fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG), a glucose analog, to detect areas of high metabolic activity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conditions like inflammation, infection, or scar tissue can mimic malignancy due to increased metabolic activity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-malignant processes that uptake FDG can lead to misinterpretation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low-grade lymphomas or indolent tumors with lower metabolic activity may not be detected effectively."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Small lesions below the resolution limit of the scan (<4-5 mm)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Post-Scan precautions:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Resume normal activities."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Avoid close contact with pregnant women and young children for about six hours post-scan."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Safety and Considerations:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aspect Details"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Safety Small radiation exposure; benefits in NHL diagnosis and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"management typically outweigh risks."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Considerations Discuss concerns with healthcare providers before the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"procedure."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bone scans (Option A) : Bone scans are not the primary imaging modality for NHL, which involves soft tissues and lymph nodes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gallium-67 scans (Option C) : While previously used, PET scans have largely replaced Gallium-67 scans for staging and response evaluation in lymphoma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tc-99m tilmanocept scans (Option D) : These scans are typically used in sentinel lymph node mapping and are not relevant for NHL management."}],"qi":[7237]},{"n":12,"q":"Which of the following is the most likely complication associated with the above intravenous access system?","o":[{"t":"Thrombosis"},{"t":"Bloodstream infection"},{"t":"Device fracture"},{"t":"Air embolism"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The above given image is of the Hickman Catheter. Hickman line may be inserted to provide access to the circulation for chemotherapy, fluids, blood products and other supportive drugs."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The most frequent complication of Hickman catheters is due to external components and the need for regular flushing."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hickman Catheters:"},{"k":"img","v":7243},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thrombosis (Option A): Can occur but is less frequent than infections."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Device fracture (Option C): Rare, especially in comparison to infections."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Air embolism (Option D): Possible but uncommon, and not the most likely complication."}],"qi":[7243]},{"n":13,"q":"A 50-year-old man diagnosed with chronic ITP 6 months ago presents with recurrent oral mucosal bleeding despite treatment with prednisolone. His platelet count is 45,000/µL. He is not a candidate for splenectomy. Which of the following is the most appropriate next-line therapy?","o":[{"t":"A) Eltrombopag"},{"t":"B) Intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIgG)"},{"t":"C) Platelet transfusion"},{"t":"D) High-dose prednisolone"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Eltrombopag is a thrombopoietin receptor agonist (TPO-RA) that stimulates platelet production in the bone marrow by increasing the proliferation and differentiation of megakaryocytes. It is often used as a second-line therapy in patients with chronic ITP who are refractory to corticosteroids and who are not candidates for splenectomy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immune thrombocytopenic purpura/ idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Most common in adults"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Type 2 hypersensitivity reaction"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"females: males= 3:1"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"auto antibodies against GPIb-IX/ GPIIb-IIIa"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immune-mediated destruction of platelets"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical manifestation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral mucosal, gastrointestinal andMucocutaneous bleeding,"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"heavy menstrual bleeding"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wet purpura"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Retinal haemorrhages"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thrombocytopenia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peripheral blood smear: Reduced platelet count and large platelets (immature platelets>6%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bone marrow examination: Increase in megakaryocytes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Evaluation for secondary causes of ITP"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Splenectomy (even though there is no splenomegaly)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In relapse/contraindication of splenectomy Thrombopoietin receptor agonists (TPO-RA): Romiplostim, eltrombopag. (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thrombopoietin receptor agonists (TPO-RA): Romiplostim, eltrombopag. (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immunosuppressive therapy: Rituximab"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thrombopoietin receptor agonists (TPO-RA): Romiplostim, eltrombopag. (Option A)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIgG) (Option B): It can temporarily increase platelet counts in patients with ITP by inhibiting the destruction of platelets. It is usually used in acute ITP, where a rapid increase in platelet count is needed. Since this patient has chronic ITP and is not in a life-threatening situation, IVIgG is less suitable for treatment compared to options like Eltrombopag."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Platelet transfusion (Option C): It is not effective in patients with ITP because the immune system continues to destroy the transfused platelets as it does with the patient’s own platelets. It is reserved for cases of life-threatening bleeding or emergency situations, not for routine management of chronic ITP. This option is incorrect."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High-dose prednisolone (Option D): This patient has already been on prednisolone, and the persistence of symptoms suggests that he is refractory to corticosteroid treatment. Escalating the dose of prednisolone is not advisable due to potential adverse effects."}]},{"n":14,"q":"A 55-year-old woman presents with localised bone pain and swelling in her left femur. A biopsy confirms the presence of abnormal plasma cells. Based on this presentation, which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Multiple myeloma"},{"t":"Solitary plasmacytoma of bone"},{"t":"Extramedullary plasmacytoma"},{"t":"Osteosarcoma"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient’s presentation of a solitary bone lesion with abnormal plasma cells is characteristic of solitary plasmacytoma, a localised tumour of plasma cells."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Plasmacytoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Localised plasma cell tumour in bone or soft tissue."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typically presents as a solitary mass without systemic disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Types of Plasmacytoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Solitary Plasmacytoma of Bone (SPB) : Single lesion in bone marrow."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extramedullary Plasmacytoma (EMP) : Originates in mucosal tissues (e.g., respiratory or gastrointestinal tract)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intermediate phase between MGUS and multiple myeloma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Factors like inflammation or trauma increase stromal cell-derived factor 1 (SDF-1), promoting plasma cell proliferation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Laboratory Studies : Detection of monoclonal proteins in serum/urine (50% may not show M-protein)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Histology : Lesional biopsy or fine-needle aspiration (fluoroscopic or CT-guided biopsies for spinal lesions)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Indication"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Radiation Therapy Primary treatment for solitary plasmacytomas"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Chemotherapy Considered if risk of progression to multiple myeloma"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Surgical Intervention May be indicated for tumour removal"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Prognosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Varies based on histological grade and angiogenesis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased angiogenesis is linked to a higher risk of progression to multiple myeloma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Regular monitoring is essential for early detection of progression."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Multiple myeloma (Option A) : This diagnosis would involve widespread proliferation of plasma cells in the bone marrow, rather than a solitary lesion."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Extramedullary plasmacytoma (Option C) : This variant occurs in soft tissues, not in the bone, making it less likely in this case."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Osteosarcoma (Option D) : Although it can present as a bone lesion, the histological findings of abnormal plasma cells confirm the diagnosis as plasmacytoma rather than a primary bone malignancy."}],"qi":[7249,7254]},{"n":15,"q":"A 56-year-old male presents with lymphadenopathy, fatigue, and weight loss. A peripheral blood smear reveals atypical lymphocytes with cleaved, lobulated nuclei resembling as shown below. Based on the histological findings, what is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)"},{"t":"Adult T-cell Leukemia-Lymphoma (ATLL)"},{"t":"Hodgkin's Lymphoma"},{"t":"Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The presence of atypical lymphocytes with cleaved, lobulated nuclei is characteristic of cloverleaf cells, which are associated with ATLL."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cloverleaf Appearance in Histology"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Refers to atypical lymphocytes with cleaved, lobulated nuclei."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Commonly observed in hematological malignancies, particularly adult T-cell leukemia-lymphoma (ATLL)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Histopathology features:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Feature Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Nuclear Structure Cleaved and lobulated nuclei resembling a cloverleaf;"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"seen in atypical lymphocytes (e.g., ATLL)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cytoplasm Basophilic, larger than normal lymphocytes (twice the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"size of an erythrocyte)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Peripheral Blood Shows cloverleaf cells with atypical nuclear morphology Smear and increased size in ATLL."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Bone Marrow Analysis Reveals atypical lymphocytes, useful for diagnosis and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"staging of ATLL."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Picture"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Associated Condition Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Adult T-cell Leukemia-Lymph Associated with HTLV-1 infection; presents with oma (ATLL) leukocytosis,Tax gene +, thrombocytosis, and elevated"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"serum lactate dehydrogenase levels. Imaging may show multifocal lymphadenopathy."},{"k":"img","v":7352},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Symptoms and Signs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphadenopathy : Swollen lymph nodes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fatigue : General tiredness."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weight Loss : Unintentional loss of body weight."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Skin Lesions : Manifestations vary based on disease extent."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Laboratory Findings Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Peripheral Blood Smears Identification of cloverleaf cells (CD3+ T- lymphocytes)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"HTLV-1 Testing Confirmation of HTLV-1 presence via serological testing."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Flow Cytometry Assists in characterizing the T-cell population involved in"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ATLL."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) (Option A) : Typically shows small, mature lymphocytes with a characteristic \"smudge cell\" appearance, not cloverleaf cells."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hodgkin's Lymphoma (Option C) : Usually characterized by Reed-Sternberg cells and does not present with cloverleaf cell morphology."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma (Option D) : This is a broad category that encompasses many types of lymphomas, but cloverleaf cells specifically point towards ATLL."}],"qi":[7260]},{"n":16,"q":"A 35-year-old male presents with fatigue, weakness, and pallor. A peripheral blood smear reveals red blood cells that are smaller than normal and exhibit a decreased color saturation, with a prominent central pallor noted in the histological examination as shown below. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Thalassemia"},{"t":"Sideroblastic anemia"},{"t":"Iron deficiency"},{"t":"Chronic disease"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Iron deficiency anemia is characterized by the presence of microcytic hypochromic red blood cells, which appear smaller and paler due to insufficient hemoglobin content . This results in a prominent central pallor."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of Microcytic Microchromic Anemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cause Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Iron Deficiency Most common cause ; iron is essential for hemoglobin"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"synthesis, and its deficiency leads to decreased hemoglobin production , resulting in microcytic, hypochromic red blood cells. deficiency anemia- peripheral smear\" data-author=\"Ed Uthman\" data-hash=\"9755\" data-license=\"CC BY 2.0\" data-source=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iro n-deficiency_Anemia,_Peripheral_Blood_Smear_%2844 22704616%29.jpg#/media/File:Iron-deficiency_Anemia,_ Peripheral_Blood_Smear_(4422704616).jpg\" data-tags=\"April2025\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com /notes/Q6oPG4JNzpNWeVui8F361744415830.png\" />"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Thalassemia Genetic disorder affecting globin chain synthesis,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"leading to imbalanced globin production and impaired hemoglobin synthesis , resulting in smaller, paler red blood cells. (Option A ruled out) thalassemia minor\" data-author=\"Khera, Rachna & Singh, Tejinder. \" data-hash=\"9747\" data-license=\"ResearchGate GmbH. All rights reserved.\" data-source=\"https://www.researchgate.net/fig ure/b-Thalassemia-minor-Peripheral-smear-showing-mild -anisopoikilocytosis-and-microcytic_fig5_273835999\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"263\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"CvcjZdV01744410489.png\" width=\"400\" />"},{"k":"img","v":7359},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Sideroblastic Anemia"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations for Microcytic Microchromic Anemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood Film and Reticulocyte Count : Essential for initial assessment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum Ferritin Levels : Evaluates iron stores if hypochromia is present."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemoglobin Electrophoresis : Differentiates between iron deficiency anemia and thalassemia by assessing hemoglobin A2 levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bone Marrow Examination : Considered if hemoglobin levels are below 80 g/L for definitive diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anemia of chronic disease (Option D): This typically presents as normocytic anemia with normal-sized red blood cells, making it an unlikely diagnosis in this case."}],"qi":[7262]},{"n":17,"q":"A 65-year-old male patient presents with bone pain, anemia, and increased susceptibility to infections. A bone marrow biopsy reveals hypercellularity with the infiltration of plasma cells exhibiting a distinctive fiery red cytoplasm and a prominent Golgi apparatus as shown below. Immunohistochemical staining shows positivity for CD38 and CD138. What is the most likely diagnosis for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"},{"t":"Multiple myeloma"},{"t":"Lymphoma"},{"t":"Acute myeloid leukemia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The findings of hypercellularity with a predominance of plasma cells, especially those characterized as flame cells, along with the patient's symptoms of bone pain, anemia, and infection risk, strongly suggest a diagnosis of multiple myeloma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Flame Cells in Hematology"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Definition : Morphological variant of plasma cells, commonly associated with multiple myeloma."},{"k":"img","v":7268},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Histopathological Findings"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bone Marrow Biopsy : Hypercellular marrow with diffuse plasma cell infiltration, including flame cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypercellular marrow with diffuse plasma cell infiltration, including flame cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immunohistochemistry : Positive for plasma cell markers (CD38 and CD138), aiding in differentiation from other cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Positive for plasma cell markers (CD38 and CD138), aiding in differentiation from other cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypercellular marrow with diffuse plasma cell infiltration, including flame cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Positive for plasma cell markers (CD38 and CD138), aiding in differentiation from other cells."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Picture"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Condition Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Multiple Myeloma MM usually presents in the fifth to seventh decade of life"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"with fatigue, bone pain, anemia, and hypercalcemia Associated with flame cells; indicates a more aggressive disease course."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Eosinophilic Presence of flame figures (eosinophilic major basic Dermatosis protein deposits in collagen) linked to hematologic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"malignancies; characterized by pruritic, erythematous papules and vesicular lesions."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis and Monitoring"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Detection of flame cells in bone marrow aspirates or biopsies is crucial for diagnosing multiple myeloma and assessing disease progression and treatment response."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (Option A) : Chronic lymphocytic leukemia primarily involves small, mature lymphocytes, not plasma cells, and is characterized by different clinical features."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lymphoma (Option C) : Lymphoma generally presents with lymphadenopathy and does not typically show the same plasma cell morphology as described."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute myeloid leukemia (Option D) : Acute myeloid leukemia is characterized by myeloid blasts, not plasma cells, and presents with different clinical symptoms."}],"qi":[7268]},{"n":18,"q":"A 6-month-old child presents with severe pallor, failure to thrive, and hepatosplenomegaly. Peripheral blood smear shows microcytic hypochromic anemia, erythroblasts in circulation, and target cells. Bone marrow biopsy reveals increased cellularity with dysplastic erythroid precursors and iron-laden macrophages. Hemoglobin electrophoresis reveals elevated HbF levels. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Beta-thalassemia major"},{"t":"Iron deficiency anemia"},{"t":"Hemoglobin H disease"},{"t":"Sideroblastic anemia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Microcytic hypochromic anemia, erythroblasts in circulation, and target cells are hallmark histological findings of beta-thalassemia major"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bone marrow hypercellularity with erythroid dysplasia and iron-laden macrophages are consistent with beta-thalassemia major."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated HbF indicates compensation for deficient HbA production."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thalassemia:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Inherited condition affecting hemoglobin production."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Results from impaired synthesis of alpha or beta globin chains."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Types of Thalassemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Type Cause Prevalence"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Alpha-thalassemia Mutation in alpha gene loci on Common in Southeast Asia"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"chromosome 16 (1-4 deletions)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Beta-thalassemia Point mutations impairing beta chain Prevalent in the Mediterranean"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"synthesis (b0 or b–)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Imbalance in globin chain production leads to excess chains."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Excess chains precipitate inside RBCs, damaging membranes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Results in hemolysis and shortened RBC survival."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Beta-Thalassemia Subtypes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Subtype Features Beta-thalassemia Severe hypochromic anemia, red cell dysplasia, major erythroblastosis, high HbF"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Bone marrow: Increased cellularity with dysplastic erythroid cells and abnormal iron deposits (non-ring sideroblasts)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Beta-thalassemia Mild anemia, microcytic hypochromic RBCs (without iron minor deficiency), target cells and punctate basophilia on"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"blood smear, high HbA2"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Alpha-Thalassemia Severity (Based on Gene Deletions)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Deletions Condition Clinical Outcome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1 No clinical effect Asymptomatic"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2 Mild anemia Hypochromic RBCs"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"3 Hemoglobin H disease Moderate anemia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"4 Hydrops fetalis Fatal in utero"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hemoglobin H Disease (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insufficient alpha chains lead to formation of non-functional Hemoglobin H."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Relies on low levels of Hemoglobin A for oxygen transport."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Golf ball inclusions seen."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of Thalassemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Condition Management"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Beta-thalassemia major Regular transfusions"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Folic acid Iron chelation Splenectomy (if needed) Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hemoglobin H disease Folic acid"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Occasional transfusions Avoid iron supplements"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Picture:"},{"k":"img","v":7369},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Beta-thalassemia Major"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Beta-thalassemia Minor Asymptomatic or mild anemia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Prevention of Thalassemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Carrier screening : Identify individuals with beta-thalassemia minor."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Genetic counseling : For carrier couples."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prenatal diagnosis : Chorionic villous sampling for fetal screening."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Iron deficiency anemia (Option B): Presents with microcytic anemia but without erythroblastosis, target cells, or elevated HbF."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sideroblastic anemia (Option D): Characterized by ringed sideroblasts, not erythroid hyperplasia or elevated HbF."}],"qi":[7273]},{"n":19,"q":"A 72-year-old male presents with fatigue, melena, and frequent bruising. Physical examination reveals a systolic ejection murmur. Laboratory tests show reduced levels of high molecular weight von Willebrand factor (VWF). Which of the following interventions is most likely to reduce his gastrointestinal bleeding?","o":[{"t":"Colonoscopic resection of angiodysplastic lesions"},{"t":"Initiation of von Willebrand factor replacement therapy"},{"t":"Aortic valve replacement"},{"t":"Long-term use of proton pump inhibitors"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient likely has Heyde's syndrome. In Heyde’s syndrome, aortic stenosis causes shear stress that breaks down high molecular weight von Willebrand factor (VWF), leading to bleeding, especially from gastrointestinal angiodysplasia. Replacing the stenotic aortic valve eliminates this stress, restores normal VWF levels, and significantly reduces GI bleeding."},{"k":"img","v":7372},{"k":"img","v":7378},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Symptoms of Aortic Stenosis Symptoms of GI Bleeding Signs of Hemostasis Defects"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chest pain Hematochezia (blood in stool) Easy bruising"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Syncope Melena (black stools) Hemarthrosis (joint bleeding)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dyspnea Hematemesis (vomiting blood) Petechiae (small red spots)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fatigue due to low cardiac output Fatigue secondary to anemia Hypotension if severe bleeding"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Histopathology"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Angiodysplasia Features Vascular Changes VWF Abnormalities"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dilated, tortuous vessels Structural vessel damage from Loss of high-molecular-weight VWF"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ischemia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fragile GI vessels Hypoxia-induced changes Impaired hemostasis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Colonoscopic resection of angiodysplastic lesions (Option A): Colonoscopic resection may provide temporary relief but does not address the underlying cause."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Initiation of von Willebrand factor replacement therapy (Option B): VWF replacement can improve hemostasis temporarily but is not a definitive treatment."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Long-term use of proton pump inhibitors (Option D): Proton pump inhibitors manage upper GI bleeding but are ineffective in treating angiodysplasia linked with Heyde syndrome."}]},{"n":20,"q":"A 15-year-old girl comes to the clinic for evaluation of heavy menstrual bleeding and frequent nosebleeds. She reports having significant bruising after minor injuries and has had prolonged bleeding after dental extractions. Her family history is significant for similar bleeding tendencies among her relatives. Which of the following findings would most likely support a diagnosis of von Willebrand Disease?","o":[{"t":"Normal platelet count with prolonged aPTT"},{"t":"Prolonged PT with elevated fibrinogen"},{"t":"Normal aPTT and PT with decreased vWF levels"},{"t":"Thrombocytopenia with decreased platelet aggregation"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In von Willebrand Disease, the platelet count is usually normal, but the aPTT may be prolonged due to low Factor VIII levels. This is a key finding that supports the diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Von-Willebrand Disease"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It is the most common inherited bleeding disorder, caused by a deficiency or dysfunction of von Willebrand factor (vWF), a glycoprotein crucial for platelet adhesion and stabilizing factor VIII in the coagulation cascade."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Functions of vWF:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Platelet Adhesion: Mediates binding of platelets to subendothelial collagen at sites of vascular injury."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stabilization of Factor VIII: Protects Factor VIII from degradation, prolonging its half-life."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical manifestation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Superficial bruising, epistaxis, menorrhagia and gastrointestinal haemorrhage"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Excessive haemorrhage might only be observed after trauma or surgery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Heavy menstrual bleeding"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Might first manifest with dental extractions, particularly wisdom tooth extraction, or tonsillectomy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigation:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Typ aPTT vWF antig vWF acti FVIII acti Multimer Inheritance es en vity vity 1 Normal fl fl fl Normal distrib Autosomal"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"or › ution, decreas dominant"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ed in quantity"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"2A Normal fl flfl fl Loss of high- Autosomal"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"or › and intermedi dominant"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ate-MW multi mers"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"2B Normal fl flfl fl Loss of high- Autosomal"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"or › MW multimer dominant"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"s"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"2M Normal fl flfl fl Normal distrib Autosomal"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"or › ution, decreas dominant"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ed in quantity"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"2N ›› Normal Normal flfl Normal distrib Autosomal"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"or fl or fl ution recessive"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"3 ›› flfl flfl flfl Absent Autosomal"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"recessive"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In general, increase in aPTT and BT, but normal PT and platelet count. (Option A)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Desmopressin (DDAVP)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tranexamic acid or epsilon-aminocaproic acid for mucosal bleeding or non-serious bleeding"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Factor VIII concentrates for serious or persistent bleeding"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bleeding in type 3 patients responds only to factor VIII/vWF concentrate"}]},{"n":21,"q":"A 9-year-old boy is scheduled for a dental extraction. He has a history of recurrent epistaxis and prolonged bleeding when he is hurt. His parents are first cousins. Physical examination shows petechiae. The dentist was worried about the medical history and ordered blood investigations as follows: Tests Results aPTT, PT, and ristocetin cofactor activity Normal Bleeding time Increased Light transmission aggregometry (LTA) Altered platelet aggregation with ADP Normal aggregation with ristocetin. Flow cytometry Defective expression of alpha IIb beta 3 integrin Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment to prevent bleeding during the procedure?\nTests Results\naPTT, PT, and ristocetin cofactor activity Normal\nBleeding time Increased\nAltered platelet aggregation with ADP\nLight transmission aggregometry (LTA)\nNormal aggregation with ristocetin.\nFlow cytometry Defective expression of alpha IIb beta 3 integrin","o":[{"t":"Desmopressin"},{"t":"Tranexamic acid"},{"t":"Recombinant activated clotting factor VII (rFVIIa)"},{"t":"Fresh frozen plasma"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this scenario, the 9-year-old boy's clinical history, consanguinity, and laboratory findings are consistent with Glanzmann Thrombasthenia, a congenital platelet disorder characterized by defective platelet aggregation due to a lack of Gp IIb/IIIa receptors (aids in platelet-platelet adhesion)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"rFVIIa is helpful for patients who are undergoing surgery or are refractory to platelet transfusions as it enhances thrombin generation at the site of vascular injury and can promote hemostasis despite platelet dysfunction."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Glanzmann Thrombasthenia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Congenital bleeding disorder"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"History of high consanguinity in marriage of the parents"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Autosomal recessive inheritance"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mutations involving chromosome 17"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Absence of the platelet Gp IIb/IIIa receptor"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These receptors are essential for platelet aggregation and hemostasis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lifelong mucocutaneous bleeding episodes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Epistaxis is very common in children"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gingival bleeds"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gastrointestinal bleeding"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Menorrhagia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tests Results"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"aPTT, PT, and ristocetin cofactor activity Normal"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bleeding time Increased"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Light transmission aggregometry (LTA) (gold Altered platelet aggregation with ADP, collagen, and standard test) epinephrine."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Normal aggregation with ristocetin."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Flow cytometry Defective expression of alpha IIb beta 3 integrin"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Molecular genetic tests Gene mutations in ITGA2B or ITGB3"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Therapy Clinical setting"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Desmopressin Mild bleeding (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Platelets and/or recombinant activated clotting factor VII Surgery prophylaxis (rFVIIa)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tranexamic acid Mucocutaneous bleeding (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Recombinant FVIIa Platelet refractoriness cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Platelet transfusions have the risk of alloimmunization. So, HLA-matched single donor platelets for transfusion are preferred."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fresh frozen plasma (Option D): It is used to replace clotting factors in coagulopathy disorders (e.g., liver disease, disseminated intravascular coagulation). FFP does not contain platelets and would not address the platelet dysfunction seen in Glanzmann Thrombasthenia. This option is incorrect."}]},{"n":22,"q":"Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome is suspected in a 3-year-old boy who has eczema, gingival bleeding, and recurrent respiratory tract infections. His mother also noticed blood in his stools. What test would confirm the diagnosis of this syndrome?","o":[{"t":"A) Serum immunoelectrophoresis"},{"t":"B) Platelet function assay"},{"t":"C) Intracellular immunofluorescence"},{"t":"D) Complete blood count (CBC) with differential"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Intracellular immunofluorescence is used to detect the presence of WASP protein in blood cells. In patients with Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome, there is either a deficiency or absence of WASP protein due to mutations in the WASP gene. This test is highly specific for WAS and is commonly used to confirm the diagnosis of Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"X-linked recessive"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome is caused by mutations in the gene located at Xp11.23 that encodes Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome protein (WASP)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mutations in the WASP gene affect T lymphocytes and other lymphocyte subsets i.e. dendritic cells, and platelets"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"WASp regulates the actin cytoskeleton and thus plays an important role in many lymphocyte functions, including cell adhesion and migration and the formation of synapses between antigen-presenting and target cells"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severely dysfunctional platelets, and thrombocytopenia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immunodeficiency"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical manifestation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Recurrent bacterial infections"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Eczema"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bleeding"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Associated with IBD - Colitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased risk of development of malignant lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"IgM levels in the serum are low, but levels of IgG are usually normal. Paradoxically the levels of IgA and IgE are often elevated"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood smear reduced-sized platelets"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis is made by expression of intracellular immunofluorescence analysis of WAS protein (WASp) in blood cells. There is defective or absent WAS protein. (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prophylactic antibiotics"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immunoglobulin G (IgG) supplementation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Topical treatment of eczema"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Serum immunoelectrophoresis (Option A): This test evaluates the levels of different immunoglobulins (IgG, IgM, IgA, IgE). In Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome, there may be low levels of IgM, which supports the diagnosis of immunodeficiency. However, this test alone is not diagnostic for WAS. This option is incorrect."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Platelet function assay (Option B): In Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome, the platelets are small and dysfunctional, contributing to bleeding tendencies. While this test can show abnormal platelet function in WAS, it is not specific to confirm the diagnosis. This option is incorrect."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Complete blood count (CBC) with differential (Option D): Thrombocytopenia is seen in Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome. However, they are not specific enough to confirm the diagnosis. This option is incorrect."}]},{"n":23,"q":"A 7-year-old male child with a recent history of viral infection presents with bleeding in gums and petechiae. There is no significant past medical history. Laboratory investigations are as follows: Platelets: 15,000/µL Peripheral blood smear: Large platelets Bone marrow aspirate: Increased number of megakaryocytes Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment?","o":[{"t":"Splenectomy"},{"t":"Anti-Rh0 (D) therapy"},{"t":"Eltrombopag"},{"t":"Intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIgG)"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"IVIgG is a first-line treatment for acute ITP, especially in children, because it helps rapidly increase platelet counts by inhibiting the destruction of platelets through immune modulation. It is commonly used in cases where bleeding is present, as in this patient with gum bleeding and petechiae."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In children , it is usually an acute disease, most commonly following an infection, and with a self-limited course. In adults, it is a more chronic disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathogenesis of ITP:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute ITP\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"9740\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/xSNrI4mA12JAHxwZIzCN17444 08884.png\" />"},{"k":"img","v":7388},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment of ITP in children:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In children with newly diagnosed ITP who have non-life-threatening mucosal bleeding, the ASH guideline suggests corticosteroids (prednisolone or dexamethasone) rather than intravenous immunoglobulin or anti-D immunoglobulin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If corticosteroids are contra-indicated or otherwise not preferred, intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) can be given (Option D)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rh-positive patients: anti-Rh0 (D) therapy (antibody-coated cells “saturating” the Fc receptors, inhibiting Fc receptor function) (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Difference between acute and chronic ITP:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute and Chronic ITP\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"9743\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"356\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/3ZlYfoUAjMUbbgo1 KKtz1744409245.png\" width=\"600\" />"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of ITP:"},{"k":"img","v":7395},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Splenectomy (Option A): It is a treatment option for chronic or refractory ITP. It is not used in acute ITP as the first-line treatment, as the condition often resolves spontaneously or responds well to pharmacological therapy. This option is incorrect."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Eltrombopag (Option C): It is a thrombopoietin receptor agonist (TPO-RA) used to stimulate platelet production in patients with chronic ITP or those who are refractory to first-line treatments. It is not typically used in acute ITP, especially in children, as the condition is usually self-limiting or responsive to other treatments like IVIgG or glucocorticoids. This option is incorrect."}]}]);
